 \begin{abstract}
+\abstractspacing
 Optimization of an electron injector linac requires balancing transverse beam emittances and
-energy spread while satisfying beam transport and transmission constraints. We investigate
-Bayesian optimization of the S-band injector-linac model developed for the Korea Light Source
-using ASTRA space-charge tracking simulations. Magnetic strengths and RF phases are varied to
-minimize the horizontal and vertical normalized RMS emittances and RMS energy spread.
-Constraint-aware and unconstrained multi-objective Bayesian optimization are compared with
-weighted-sum Bayesian optimization using matched simulation budgets and shared initial designs.
+energy spread while satisfying beam transport and transmission constraints. We compare
+Bayesian optimization pipelines on a fixed-lattice S-band injector-linac model derived from the
+Korea Light Source design, using ASTRA space-charge tracking simulations. Magnetic strengths and
+RF phases are varied to minimize the horizontal and vertical normalized RMS emittances and RMS
+energy spread. Constraint-aware and unconstrained multi-objective Bayesian optimization are
+compared with weighted-sum Bayesian optimization using matched simulation budgets and shared
+initial designs. Feasibility is defined by a deliberately relaxed computational acceptance
+profile; the stricter optimization requirements of the Korea Light Source technical design are
+met by only a few sampled settings, at the edge of the explored domain, and campaigns
+constrained by them approach that region to within the numerical resolution of the model. A
+refinement check shows that the absolute emittances depend on the tracking resolution.
 All methods identify feasible nondominated operating points, revealing trade-offs between
 transverse beam quality and energy spread. Paired analyses support a higher feasible
-sampling fraction for constraint-aware acquisition, while weighted-sum optimization completes campaigns faster.
+sampling fraction for constraint-aware acquisition, which arises mainly from meeting the
+transverse beam-size and divergence limits; all pipelines sample feasible settings more often
+than a budget-matched space-filling search, while the weighted-sum implementation has the lowest
+computational overhead.
 Objective-quality comparisons depend on the indicator and comparator. A survey of
 scalarization weightings explores additional trade-offs, and a limited lattice-jitter assessment
 retains feasibility for the selected operating points. The results demonstrate the use of
 constraint-aware Bayesian optimization to identify feasible injector-linac settings and map
-beam-quality trade-offs within the stated injector model and acceptance profile.
+beam-quality trade-offs within the stated injector model and computational acceptance profile.
 \end{abstract}
 
